        "contact": {
          "url": "https://disneyapi.dev/docs/"
        },
        "description": "GET only API for Disney characters information. No authentication required. Provides endpoints to filter characters, get all characters, and get details about one character. Available in REST and GraphQL. Access to a database of 9820 Disney characters.",
        "servers": [
          {
            "description": "Production server",
            "url": "https://api.disneyapi.dev"
          }
        ],
        "termsOfService": "https://disneyapi.dev/docs/",
        "title": "Disney API",
        "version": "1.0.0",
        "tagline": "Look up Disney characters across films, shows, parks, and games via REST or GraphQL",
        "homepage": "https://disneyapi.dev",
        "docs_url": "https://disneyapi.dev",
        "license_short": "Unofficial fan-made API; licence not stated. Disney characters and names are trademarks of The Walt Disney Company.",
        "license_md": "- No licence is published on the API site; treat the service as a community resource that may change or disappear.\n- Disney character names, images, and likenesses remain trademarks/copyrights of The Walt Disney Company and its subsidiaries.\n- The API is not affiliated with or endorsed by Disney; use accordingly and check Disney's terms before any redistribution.",
        "about_md": "The [Disney API](https://disneyapi.dev) is a community-run, read-only catalogue of roughly 9,820 Disney characters drawn from Disney films, TV shows, video games, and theme-park attractions. It is served from `https://api.disneyapi.dev` and is offered free of charge with no authentication required.\n\nWhat you get from the API:\n\n- List all characters: `GET /character`\n- Filter characters by name: `GET /character?name={name}`\n- Fetch a single character by id: `GET /character/{id}`\n- Both REST and GraphQL interfaces are exposed against the same data set.\n\nThe service has CORS enabled on all endpoints and is suitable for browser-side use. No rate limits are documented, but response times are typically in the 1.5–2.5 second range, so client-side caching is recommended for any UI that pages or auto-completes against the character list.",
        "entity_desc": {
          "character": "A single Disney character record, addressable via `GET /character`, `GET /character?name={name}` for name filtering, and `GET /character/{id}` for a specific character."
        },
        "auth": false
      },
